solution:
function tally(words: readonly string[]): [string, number][] {
  const counts = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const word of words) {
    counts.set(word, (counts.get(word) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  // Map iterates in first-insertion order and sort is stable, so ties keep that order.
  return [...counts].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
}

A `Map` keys on the exact string with no inherited entries, so `'constructor'` starts at `undefined` and `?? 0` gives 0. Its iteration order is first insertion, which is exactly the tie-break rule, and since ES2019 `sort` is stable, so equal counts never swap. Spreading the Map yields a fresh array of `[key, value]` pairs, so sorting it in place never touches the input. Counting is O(n) with O(1) average Map operations and the sort is O(k log k) for k distinct words, O(n log n) overall. The plain-object starter fails twice: `counts['constructor'] ?? 0` returns the inherited `Object` function, so `+ 1` produces a string, and `Object.entries` lists integer-like keys such as `'2'` and `'10'` in ascending numeric order rather than first-seen order.

